WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.949 --> 00:00:08.320
There have been many prolific geniuses throughout
history ... Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart, Charles

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:15.889
Dickens, Vincent Van Gogh ... but legendary
film director Alan Smithee differs from these

00:00:15.889 --> 00:00:19.370
people in three important ways.

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:22.660
Firstly, he wasn’t a genius.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.590
Secondly, most of his work could be described
as “utter shit”.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:29.870
And thirdly, he doesn’t actually exist.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:36.840
Mind you, Alan never let his non-existence
hold him back, and when you consider he directed

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:43.780
such outstanding films as Ghost Fever, The
Shrimp on the Barbie, Bloodsucking Pharaohs

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:50.220
from Pittsburgh, and Le Zombi de Cap-Rouge,
it’s hard to believe he isn’t a household

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:51.220
name.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:56.600
But where did this mysterious purveyor of
mediocre movies come from?

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:04.250
Alan Smithee was born in 1969, but through
necessity rather than the birth canal, and

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:08.740
with the Directors Guild of America acting
as midwife.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:16.251
You see, Alan Smithee is simply a fake name,
a pseudonym used by members of the DGA when

00:01:16.251 --> 00:01:21.090
they were so unhappy with a movie they’d
rather burn the negative, themselves, and

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:26.670
all of their possessions before seeing their
real name attached to it.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:31.049
But you couldn’t just “Smithee” your
name out of a film willy-nilly.

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:32.450
Oh, no.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:39.090
First, you had to convince a DGA panel you’d
lost creative control of the project, and

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:44.509
even if you did get a “Yes”, you weren’t
allowed to discuss the film afterwards.

00:01:44.509 --> 00:01:49.350
In other words, the first rule of “Smithee
Club” was that you did not talk about “Smithee

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:50.350
Club”.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:53.909
People had used pseudonyms in the film industry
before, of course.

00:01:53.909 --> 00:02:01.079
In 1947, screenwriter Dalton Trumbo was blacklisted
in Hollywood on suspicion of being a communist,

00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:07.340
so he adopted various false names - including
Robert Rich and Felix Lutzkendorf - to get

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:09.300
his work out into the world.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:16.750
And in 1964, Sergio Leone made the legendary
spaghetti western “A Fistful of Dollars”

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:23.370
under the name “Bob Robertson”, thinking
it would be more palatable to American audiences.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:30.709
But it was with the 1969 film “Death of
a Gunfighter” that the most famous of all

00:02:30.709 --> 00:02:34.299
Hollywood pseudonyms first came into being.

00:02:34.299 --> 00:02:39.159
Until this point, pseudonyms were against
the DGA rules so as to stop producers from

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:43.489
forcing little-known or unpopular directors
to adopt them.

00:02:43.489 --> 00:02:49.300
The DGA were also great believers in “auteur
theory” -- which suggests that, while a

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:55.299
huge number of people come together to create
a film, the director is the driving creative

00:02:55.299 --> 00:03:00.730
force behind it, and should therefore be given
full credit for his work..

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:05.019
With “Death of a Gunfighter,” however,
things got a little complicated.

00:03:05.019 --> 00:03:10.569
Lead actor Richard Widmark went full diva
and got the original director, Robert Totten,

00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:11.569
fired.

00:03:11.569 --> 00:03:17.690
But when filming wrapped, new director Don
Siege, told a DGA panel he didn’t feel it

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:23.099
was right for him to be credited as the director,
as the film was mostly Totten’s work.

00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:29.500
But Totten himself was understandably upset
at having been unceremoniously canned from

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.909
the project, and he didn’t want his name
attached either.

00:03:32.909 --> 00:03:39.379
The DGA ultimately agreed neither director’s
creative vision had been represented in the

00:03:39.379 --> 00:03:43.450
final cut, but that left them with a problem.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:47.140
Who were they going to credit as the director?

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:52.409
Some have suggested the name “Alan Smithee”
– with its two Es -- was chosen because

00:03:52.409 --> 00:03:59.140
it’s an anagram of “The alias men” — but
this is basically an urban legend, with no

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:01.049
real evidence to back it up.

00:04:01.049 --> 00:04:03.879
The truth, sadly, is somewhat duller.

00:04:03.879 --> 00:04:09.579
“Al Smith” was the original suggestion,
but someone pointed out that picking such

00:04:09.579 --> 00:04:15.770
a common name probably wasn’t a great idea
... which turned out to be true, because several

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:21.580
“Al Smiths” were already working in the
film industry at the time… so the “Al”

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:28.950
was changed to “Alan”, the “Smith”
became “Smithee”, and a non-existent Hollywood

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:31.290
legend was born.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:37.500
Weirdly enough, Smithee’s first outing as
director was critically acclaimed, which must

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:43.130
have been more than slightly annoying for
Death of a Gunfighter’s two anonymous directors.

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:48.090
He was specifically singled out for praise,
with The New York Times writing that Alan

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:54.470
Smithee had “an adroit facility for scanning
faces and extracting sharp background detail”

00:04:54.470 --> 00:05:00.920
and Roger Ebert, for a long time the preeminent
film critic in the US, saying he admired the

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:05.450
way Smithee “allowed his story to unfold
naturally.”

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:10.820
But over the years, this would very much be
the exception that proved the rule.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:16.340
Watching an Alan Smithee film is usually the
cinematic equivalent of getting the tip of

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:20.390
your penis caught in a car door.

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:23.450
In other words, extremely painful.

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:28.410
After all, who else but Alan Smithee would
attempt a sequel to the Alfred Hitchcock classic

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:29.650
“The Birds”?

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:35.810
I mean, for Hitchcock fans, that’s akin
to catapulting your own excrement at the ceiling

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:37.930
of the Sistine Chapel.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:43.560
Even actress Tippi Hedren, who appeared in
both films, described “The Birds 2: Land’s

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:49.000
End” as “absolutely horrible” and went
on to say that it still embarrassed her decades

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.190
later.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:56.940
An IMDb rating of 2.9 suggests that embarrassment
was entirely warranted, too.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:02.850
In some cases, directors have “Smithee’d”
their movies many years after the original

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:08.680
release due to them being well and truly mangled
for television.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:14.920
When it came to the TV version of David Lynch’s
“Dune”, for example, he not only insisted

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:21.540
on an Alan Smithee directing credit, but also
changed his screenwriting credit to “Judas

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:26.711
Booth” - a combination of “Judas Iscariot”
and “John Wilkes Booth” ... and if your

00:06:26.711 --> 00:06:32.380
screenwriting pseudonym is a mixture of the
man who betrayed Christ and the man who shot

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:38.220
Abraham Lincoln, it’s probably fair to say
you’re not a big fan of the script.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:42.410
Director Michael Mann has had to give his
credits a good “Smithee-ing” on more than

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:48.160
one occasion, not least when NBC wanted to
recut his three-hour epic “Heat” back

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:50.090
in 1999.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:55.600
Mann told “Variety” that so much was cut
out of the movie it completely destroyed the

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:00.030
narrative, and he went on to describe the
edit as “Piss-poor management of an asset

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:02.530
they paid a lot of money for.”

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:08.780
Another serial credit-murderer is Martin Brest,
who felt the same way about the “in-flight”

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:13.360
versions of his films -- “Meet Joe Black”
and “Scent of a Woman” -- as the rest

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:16.250
of us feel about “in-flight” meals.

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:21.670
Needless to say, he wasted no time in “Smithee-ing”
the living daylights out of them.

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:28.060
Not all attempts by directors to “Smithee”
themselves out of their films have been successful,

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:29.210
however.

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:34.580
Director Tony Kaye did his best to disown
“American History X” after cast members

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:40.190
made changes to the script and the studio
rejected his incredibly bleak finale.

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:45.910
Kaye left in a huff, and then tried to “Alan”
his way out of a credit, which was rejected

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:52.170
by the DGA, so he tried again with another
alias: “Humpty Dumpty”.

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:59.240
Surprisingly, that didn’t work either, so
he sued the DGA for two-hundred-million dollars

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:03.320
and alienated himself from everyone in Hollywood
in the process.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:09.610
He’s also reported to have engaged in some
pretty bizarre behaviour during this time,

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:16.350
like turning up to a meeting with the film
studio accompanied by a rabbi, a Tibetan monk,

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:17.720
and a priest.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:21.360
Which sounds like the start of an inappropriate
joke your uncle tells when he’s had too

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:26.270
much to drink, but apparently Kaye was deadly
serious, feeling he needed the help of God

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:27.680
to get his point across.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:29.311
I suppose that’s Hollywood for you.

00:08:29.311 --> 00:08:32.280
You never have that kind of trouble running
a YouTube channel.

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:39.000
Possibly one of the most ironic uses of Hollywood’s
favourite pseudonym was in 1997’s “An

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:45.570
Alan Smithee Film: Burn Hollywood Burn,”
a mockumentary with Eric Idle playing a director

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:52.240
named “Alan Smithee” who tries to disown
his big budget action film starring Sylvester

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:55.810
Stallone, Whoopi Goldberg, and Jackie Chan.

00:08:55.810 --> 00:09:00.990
Needless to say, he can’t “Alan Smithee”
himself out of the credits, because that’s

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:05.950
already his name, so he’s forced to steal
the film and go on the run.

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:10.980
Which is all confusing enough as it, before
you factor in what happened after production.

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:17.370
You see, director Arthur Hiller, like many
directors before him, complained to the DGA

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:23.510
that he’d been kept out of the editing process
and should therefore be able to abstain from

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:28.970
a credit on the film... all of which means
that a film about Alan Smithee ... called

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:35.590
“An Alan Smithee Film: Burn Hollywood Burn”
... ended up being directed by Alan Smithee

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:36.590
himself.

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:41.600
You just couldn’t make it up - which is
pretty much how Hiller felt.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:46.160
To add insult to injury, the film was a massive
failure, which probably shouldn’t come as

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:51.330
too much of a surprise considering the entire
plot was based on an obscure piece of Hollywood

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:56.770
culture the vast majority of the population
has never even heard of.

00:09:56.770 --> 00:10:01.530
It grossed just under sixty-thousand dollars
worldwide, despite having an estimated budget

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:05.800
of ten million, and was absolutely crucified
by the critics.

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:10.830
With a weird sense of coming full circle,
Roger Ebert -- who’d written such a positive

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:19.840
review of “Death of a Gunfighter” back
in 1969 -- gave it a score of zero stars.

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:25.890
It has a Rotten Tomatoes rating of eight percent
positive, and their critical consensus calls

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:32.400
it "A witless Hollywood satire whose hammy,
obvious jokes are neither funny nor insightful

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:34.040
of the movie business.”

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:37.530
Pretty damning, but then I suppose it’s
their job to be critical.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:41.980
When your lead actor’s saying a similar
thing, however, then it’s probably not a

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:43.620
great sign.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:48.050
Even when he was busy promoting the film,
Eric Idle regularly came out with the phrase:

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:53.550
“This is rather dreadful” ... If it was
possible for actors to Smithee themselves

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:58.470
out of a film, you can bet Idle would have
done so, even if he was portraying a character

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:00.010
called Alan Smithee.

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:06.380
“Burn Hollywood Burn” won four “Razzies”
– or Golden Raspberry awards – for Worst

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:13.630
Picture, Worst Screenplay, Worst New Star,
and Worst Supporting Actor. all which went

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:18.490
to Joe Eszterhas, who both starred in and
produced the film.

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:22.240
Sylvestor Stallone was also nominated in the
‘worst supporting actor’ category that

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:28.690
year, which is particularly ironic when you
consider he was playing himself.

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:34.590
You know your acting career’s in trouble
when you can’t even portray yourself convincingly.

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:39.931
Eszterhas also won the “Stinker” award
for Worst On-Screen Hairstyle, and the film

00:11:39.931 --> 00:11:45.690
was nominated for another Stinker in the category
of “Most Painfully Unfunny Comedy” ... so,

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:49.470
yeah, it’s fair to say it wasn’t a great
piece of work.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:54.621
But perhaps its most damning legacy of all
was that even Alan Smithee couldn’t get

00:11:54.621 --> 00:11:56.310
work afterwards.

00:11:56.310 --> 00:12:00.990
The negativity surrounding the film had brought
so much attention to the pseudonym it could

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:03.780
no longer be used at all.

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:09.430
Filmmakers have used a variety of other pseudonyms
over the years, but for comedy value none

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:12.860
are quite as good as screenwriter Robert Towne’s.

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:18.320
After his passion project -- 1984’s “Greystoke:
The Legend of Tarzan, Lord of the Apes”

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:23.830
-- was destroyed by studio interference he
removed his writing credit and replaced it

00:12:23.830 --> 00:12:29.550
with “P.H Vazak” … which might sound
exotic, but was actually the name of his pet

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:30.779
sheepdog.

00:12:30.779 --> 00:12:36.710
This not only highlighted the fact that more
dogs should have surnames, but also resulted

00:12:36.710 --> 00:12:44.930
in an Academy Award nomination for the screenplay,
making P.H the world’s first and only canine

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:46.850
to be considered for an Oscar.

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:48.480
Good boy.

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:53.170
Animal pseudonyms seem to be popular within
the screenwriting community, because fantasy

00:12:53.170 --> 00:12:58.890
writer Harlan Ellison occasionally used the
name “Cordwainer Bird” when his work was

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:01.060
mangled by producers.

00:13:01.060 --> 00:13:05.670
Fellow writer Stephen King thought this was
Ellison’s subtle way of flipping studio

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:08.000
execs “the bird”.

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:13.590
Here in the UK, we briefly had our own version
of Alan Smithee, “David Agnew”, a name

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:18.680
that was invented to overcome a very British
problem: Bureaucracy.

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:23.420
This pseudonym was used by the BBC during
the 70s if freelance writers were unable to

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:28.529
incorporate changes requested by a show’s
producer at the last minute.

00:13:28.529 --> 00:13:32.530
When that happened, members of the production
staff — who weren’t allowed to take screen

00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:37.550
credits without a long, drawn-out appeals
process — would be asked to perform a rewrite

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:39.790
under Agnew’s name.

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:45.839
One notable example is the 1979 Doctor Who
episode “City of Death”, not least because

00:13:45.839 --> 00:13:50.320
it was co-written by Hitchhiker’s Guide
to the Galaxy author Douglas Adams.

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:54.110
If you aren’t familiar with Adams’ work,
I encourage you to go and check it out.

00:13:54.110 --> 00:13:59.930
That guy really does know a thing or two about
life, the universe, and everything.

00:13:59.930 --> 00:14:05.550
Anyway, David Agnew has since become a familiar
name in Doctor Who fandom, occasionally popping

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:10.879
up in other episodes and books, not to mention
the mockumentary “The Elusive David Agnew”

00:14:10.879 --> 00:14:16.149
... which, by the way, was directed by some
guy called “Alan Smithee”.

00:14:16.149 --> 00:14:20.620
Back on the other side of the Atlantic, Alan
has also enjoyed a thriving career outside

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:22.399
of motion pictures.

00:14:22.399 --> 00:14:27.850
He directed two episodes of MacGyver, a few
segments of Tiny Toon Adventures, and a couple

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:32.610
of episodes of The Cosby Show ... although
to be fair, it isn’t only disgruntled directors

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:35.920
who want to distance themselves from Bill
Cosby these days.

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:41.220
Smithee’s also written several comics for
DC and Marvel, and has even dipped his toe

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:42.910
into the world of gaming.

00:14:42.910 --> 00:14:50.250
The 1993 Nintendo game “Equinox” credits
Alan Smithee as director, “Marine Sharpshooter

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:56.680
4” has him as art director, and in a weird
twist, Alan showed up as the director of “Metal

00:14:56.680 --> 00:15:02.880
Gear Solid 4” in a teaser trailer before
being replaced by Hideo Kojima at launch.

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:07.600
Is it possible that Alan Smithee requested
his name be removed from the project?

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:13.100
Maybe he didn’t want such a critically acclaimed
game ruining his hard-fought reputation as

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:16.080
one of the worst directors ever to have lived.

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:19.730
Or ever to have not lived, in his case.

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:24.970
Like many famous directors before him, Alan’s
also worked on quite a few music videos in

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:26.210
his time.

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:30.270
There was Whitney Houston’s “I Will Always
Love You”, “Kiss the Rain,” by Billie

00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:34.940
Myers, “Waiting for Tonight” by Jennifer
Lopez, “Maria” by Blondie, and “Lose

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:40.430
My Breath” by Destiny’s Child ... but
despite his glittering career in music promos,

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:45.370
there doesn’t seem to be an official “Alan
Smithee” for the musicians themselves - which

00:15:45.370 --> 00:15:51.230
is a shame, because some of them seem pretty
keen to disown their most famous works.

00:15:51.230 --> 00:15:56.310
Radiohead have long since grown tired of their
signature song “Creep”, going so far as

00:15:56.310 --> 00:16:01.300
to pull it from their live set for seven years;
Led Zeppelin’s Robert Plant once pledged

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:06.400
ten-thousand dollars to a radio station if
they agreed never to play “Stairway to Heaven”

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:13.040
again; and, in 2008, Madonna publicly stated
she wouldn’t sing “Like a Virgin” or

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:17.900
“Holiday” ever again unless somebody paid
her “thirty million dollars”.

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:23.700
Mind you, she was singing them eight years
later on her 2016 tour, so I guess someone

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:25.160
put up the cash.

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:28.540
So much for artistic integrity, eh, Madge?

00:16:28.540 --> 00:16:36.560
After some thirty years at the top, Alan Smithee’s
career was officially over by the year 2000

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:41.700
following director Walter Hill’s attempts
to get his film “Supernova” off the ground.

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:46.970
Hill’s budget was not only cut by the studio
halfway through production, but they also

00:16:46.970 --> 00:16:52.570
decided to preview the movie before any of
the effects had been added.

00:16:52.570 --> 00:16:55.360
Not a great choice for a sci-fi film.

00:16:55.360 --> 00:17:00.550
Anyway, Hill soon left the project, but due
to the backlash from “Burn Hollywood Burn”,

00:17:00.550 --> 00:17:07.360
his name wasn’t replaced by “Alan Smithee”
but by “Thomas Lee”; a new name the DGA

00:17:07.360 --> 00:17:08.640
were trying out.

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:12.819
The great Alan Smithee was officially dead.

00:17:12.819 --> 00:17:19.699
But despite the significant handicap of being
deceased, Alan still pops up every now and

00:17:19.699 --> 00:17:22.539
then, albeit with various creative tweaks.

00:17:22.539 --> 00:17:29.580
The duo of “Alan and Alana Smithee” wrote
the screenplay for the film “Hidden 3D”,

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:34.950
and in 2010, a Dutch, female version of Alan
-- “Ellen Smith” – most likely to meet

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:39.750
their imaginary employees diversity quota
-- directed the film “Eep!”.

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:47.549
And then, in 2018, defying all medical expectations,
the late Alan Smithee himself directed the

00:17:47.549 --> 00:17:52.789
horror movie “The Night Watchers”, along
with several episodes of the French TV series

00:17:52.789 --> 00:17:54.690
“Tomorrow is ours”.

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:57.500
For a dead guy, he’s been surprisingly busy.

00:17:57.500 --> 00:18:02.750
But, hey, Alan’s never let a little thing
like not existing get in the way of his work

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:06.860
… and isn’t it about time Hollywood recognised
that fact with some kind of award?

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:10.549
After all, who else can you think of with
such an extensive IMDB listing?

00:18:10.549 --> 00:18:16.679
Let me leave you with this thought: Screenwriter
Charlie Kaufman was nominated for an Oscar

00:18:16.679 --> 00:18:22.999
for the 2002 film “Adaptation”, along
with his “late” twin brother Donald.

00:18:22.999 --> 00:18:27.330
The weird thing, however, was that Donald
hadn’t died.

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:30.320
In fact, he’d never lived.

00:18:30.320 --> 00:18:35.559
Kaufman just invented him to add depth to
his multi-dimensional plot, and decided to

00:18:35.559 --> 00:18:41.450
give him a screenwriting credit ... so if
Charlie’s non-existent Donald can be nominated

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:45.059
for an Oscar ... then why can’t our non-existent
Alan?

00:18:45.059 --> 00:18:49.690
He doesn’t have to win, of course ... and
he probably won’t, because he’s directed

00:18:49.690 --> 00:19:09.210
some of the worst films in history ... but
hey, it’s the thought that counts.

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:11.600
Thanks for watching.

